course: everyday
lesson: 317
title: Teeth, baths and showers, safety glasses, getting dressed, sleep and safe food
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Small everyday habits keep your body safe: the right tool for your teeth, an adult near a baby in the bath, safety glasses for experiments, and clothes put on in the right order.
explain: Sleep needs the right place and the right time. A dark, quiet, cool bedroom and no screens for about an hour before bed help you fall asleep and sleep deeply.
explain: Food can be safe or risky. Fruit is washed before eating, lunches are shared only when nobody has an allergy and adults agree, and a healthy snack keeps your energy steady between meals.
explain: A whole grape is just the right size and shape to block a toddler's small airway. That is why grapes are cut lengthways for little brothers and sisters.
fact: To get food out from between your teeth, use dental floss, never a fork, a pin or any sharp object, because sharp things cut the gums and chip teeth.
fact: Never leave a small child alone in the bath, even for a moment; if you have to go, call an adult.
fact: Small children can drown in a few centimetres of water in a few seconds, and drowning is often silent.
fact: Keeping showers short, about five minutes, saves water and the energy used to heat it.
fact: Wear safety glasses when you are told to, for example when splashing chemicals or doing experiments, because eyes can be hurt for life.
fact: Get dressed in order: underwear first, then your clothes, then socks and shoes; it is quicker and clothes fit right.
fact: Put tablets and phones away about an hour before sleep and keep them out of the bedroom, because their light and excitement make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: A dark, quiet and cool bedroom helps deep sleep.
fact: Chicken must be cooked right through with no pink inside, because undercooked chicken can carry germs that make you very ill.
fact: Wash fruit and vegetables from the shop before eating them, because they may have dirt or chemicals on them.
fact: Share food from a friend's lunch only if neither of you has allergies and adults agree, because some foods are dangerous for people with allergies.
fact: When you are hungry between meals, a healthy snack like fruit, yoghurt or nuts (if you are not allergic) keeps your energy steady.
fact: Cut grapes lengthways for small children and do not give them hard sweets, because small round food can block a small child's airway.
exercise: A bit of meat is stuck between your teeth. What should you use to get it out? | Dental floss | The point of a fork | A sewing pin | A paper clip
exercise: Why should you never pick your teeth with a pin or fork? | Sharp things cut the gums and chip teeth | Metal from pins and forks slowly turns your teeth yellow | It is only bad manners at the table | Pins are fine if they are clean
exercise: Your mum asks you to watch your two-year-old brother in the bath, and then the doorbell rings. What should you do? | Stay with him and call an adult to get the door | Run to the door, he will be fine for a minute | Leave him and tell him to sit still | Turn the tap on more so he can play
exercise: Why must a small child never be left alone in the bath? | They can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds | The water will get cold too quickly and they will catch a chill | They might use too much soap | They will get bored and cry
exercise: A boy stays in the shower for half an hour every morning. What is a better habit? | Keep showers short, about five minutes | Have two long showers instead of one | Leave the water running while he dries | Use only cold water but stay as long
exercise: Why is a short shower a good idea? | It saves water and the energy to heat it | Long showers make your skin too clean | Short showers stop you catching colds | Water costs nothing, so it is just faster
exercise: In a science lesson, the teacher hands out safety glasses before you mix vinegar and baking soda. What should you do? | Put them on and keep them on | Wear them on your head like a hairband | Skip them, vinegar is only from the kitchen | Wear them only if you see others do
exercise: Why are safety glasses worn for experiments? | Splashes can hurt your eyes for life | They help you see small things better | They are part of a lab uniform only | They keep your hair out of the way
exercise: In what order should you get dressed? | Underwear, then clothes, then socks and shoes | Shoes first, then clothes, then underwear | Socks and shoes, then underwear, then clothes | Any order works just as well
exercise: A girl watches videos on her tablet in bed until late and cannot fall asleep. What should she do? | Put the tablet away an hour before bed, outside the bedroom | Turn the screen brightness down a little and keep watching until she feels sleepy | Watch calmer videos until midnight | Keep it under her pillow in case she wakes
exercise: Why do screens at bedtime make sleep harder? | Their light and excitement keep the brain awake | Screens make the bedroom air too cold and dry to sleep | They use up the body's energy | They only matter for grown-ups
exercise: A boy's bedroom is hot, bright from a street lamp and noisy. Which change will help him sleep best? | Make it dark, quiet and cool | Turn the heating up and open the curtains | Put on loud music to cover the noise | Leave the big light on all night
exercise: You buy apples from the shop and want to eat one now. What should you do first? | Wash it under clean water | Nothing, shop fruit is always clean | Rub it on your sleeve | Peel off just the sticker
exercise: At lunch your friend offers you half of her peanut butter sandwich. When is it all right to share? | Only if neither of you has allergies and adults agree | Always, because sharing food with friends is the kind thing to do | Only if you swap something back | Whenever the teacher is not looking
exercise: You are hungry an hour before dinner. Which snack is best? | An apple or a pot of yoghurt | A big bag of sweets | A fizzy drink | Nothing, wait and eat double at dinner
exercise: Your mum is giving your little sister grapes. What is the safe way to serve them? | Cut lengthways into smaller pieces | Whole, so she can suck on them | Frozen whole, as a treat | With hard sweets mixed in
exercise: Why are whole grapes and hard sweets dangerous for toddlers? | Small round food can block a small child's airway | They have far too much sugar in them for a baby's tummy | They make toddlers too sleepy | They are too sour for small children
test: At a birthday party, a girl sees a toddler about to eat a whole grape. What should she do? | Tell an adult, so it can be cut into small pieces | Let him eat it, grapes are healthy food and good for him | Give him a hard sweet instead | Tell him to chew quickly
test: A boy uses a toothpick-sharp pencil to clean his teeth and his gum starts bleeding. What would have been better? | Dental floss | A sharper pencil | A knife from the kitchen | A fingernail pressed hard
test: Your baby cousin is splashing in a shallow bath and your aunt goes to answer the phone. Which is TRUE? | Someone must stay with the baby, because even shallow water can drown a small child | It is safe, because the water is not deep enough to cover the baby's face | It is safe if the bath has toys in it | Babies float, so they cannot drown
test: A family wants to use less water. Which habit helps? | Taking five-minute showers | Taking very long baths every day | Leaving the tap running while brushing | Washing every cup under running water
test: Your friend says, «I'll just mix the cleaning stuff in the jar, I don't need goggles.» What is TRUE? | Eyes can be hurt for life by a splash | Goggles are only for swimming | Only adults' eyes can be hurt | Blinking fast protects your eyes
test: A girl puts on her shoes, then tries to pull her trousers on over them. What would help? | Underwear, then clothes, then socks and shoes | Wear bigger trousers | Put the shoes on last but socks first | Leave the shoes on and skip the trousers
test: Two boys go to bed at the same time. One reads a paper book; the other plays a game on his phone. Who will probably fall asleep faster? | The one reading the book | The one playing on the phone | Both exactly the same | It depends only on what they ate
test: A girl in Leeds swaps half her lunch with a new classmate she hardly knows. Why should she have checked first? | Her classmate might be allergic to something in it | Swapping lunches with someone she doesn't know is against the law | Her lunch might be cold | The classmate might want more
test: Strawberries from the market look clean. Which is TRUE? | They should still be washed, because they may have dirt or chemicals | Clean-looking fruit never needs washing, because the market already washes it | Only bananas need washing | Washing takes the vitamins away
test: Which bedroom is best for deep sleep? | A dark, quiet and cool room | A bright, warm room with the TV on | A noisy room with the door open | A hot room with a phone by the pillow
explain: A dad in Ohio stepped out of the bathroom for a towel while his baby was in the bath; when he came back she was under the water, and only quick first aid saved her.
explain: A girl in Wales skipped her safety glasses in a science club experiment; a splash of vinegar went in her eye and stung for hours.
fact: Drowning in the bath can happen in a few centimetres of water and in less than a minute.
fact: Chemicals and flying bits can damage the eye in an instant, and safety glasses stop most of these injuries.
test: baby's in the bath and the door bell went | Take the baby out or stay; never leave her | Run to the door quickly | Tell her to sit still | Leave a toy to keep her busy
test: safety glasses are so uncool, skip em | Wear them; eyes are easily damaged | Skip them for small experiments | Wear sunglasses instead | Close your eyes when it splashes
test: chicken looks a bit pink inside, eat it anyway? | No, undercooked chicken can make you very ill | Yes, pink is fine | Yes, if it's hot | Yes, if you're hungry
test: did u hear a kid did a science thing at home without goggles and "got stuff in his eye, can't see now". lesson? | Wear safety glasses when told; eyes can be hurt for life | Goggles only matter at school | Blink fast and it's fine | Only acids can hurt eyes
test: my mum's friend says a toddler "drowned in a bucket of water in 2 seconds". what's the true bit | Small children can drown quickly and silently in a little water | Toddlers can't drown in shallow water, coz they just stand up | Only pools are dangerous | Drowning is always loud
test: kids are saying Ben "stinks cos he never showers". pass it on? | No, it's mean and probably untrue; don't spread it | Yes, he stinks | Yes, and spray him with deodorant | Yes, tell the teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the danger posed by bath water to small children? | Drowning may occur in a few centimetres of water, often silently | Bath water is safe so long as it stays below the height of the child's knee | Children always cry out when slipping | Drowning occurs only in deep water
exam: Identify the principal reason for which fruit and vegetables from the shop are washed before eating. | They may carry dirt or chemicals | Washing makes them sweeter | Washing preserves their colour | Unwashed fruit is illegal to eat
exam: It is given that a pupil is instructed to wear safety glasses. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | They shall be worn, since they prevent most eye injuries | They may be removed if they prove uncomfortable or steam up | They are needed only for adults | They are worn only for photographs
exam: State the conditions of a bedroom that assist deep sleep. | Dark, quiet and cool | Bright, noisy and warm | Lit by a screen until sleep | Warm with the television on
